  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nWe have crystal clear sky cover this morning, and with sfc winds \ngoing calm, we are beginning to increase the effective radiational \ncooling this morning. Frost and freeze headlines continue as is for \nthis morning, and plan for them to expire on time at 13z. For today, \nplenty of sunshine expected again, with winds mainly from the east \nas the center of the sfc high sits over the northeastern US. Even \nthough we have a chilly start today, temps should be able to rebound \ninto the mid 60s by this afternoon due to weaker CAA than yesterday. \n\nThe forecast remains dry and seasonal for the entire week ahead. The \nupper trough that passed through yesterday will begin to deepen into \nan upper closed low over the East Coast today, and will stall out \nover the western Atlantic for several days. The upper pattern jet \nstreaks will remain across southern Canada in the days ahead, which \nmeans CONUS will lack any momentum to eradicate the stagnant upper \nlow from the mid-Atlantic region. Upstream ridging will amplify over \nthe next several days, with sfc high pressure sitting over a large \nportion of the country for the week. \n\nThe upper ridge amplifies even more by mid to late week as an PNW \nupper low shifts south over California, resulting in an Omega \npattern to take shape from sea to shining sea. This keeps us dry \nthrough next weekend, with daily temps climbing back into the 70s. \nBy next weekend, the amplified ridge axis should be over the Ohio \nValley, with a 1030+ mb sfc high aiding in temps potentially \nreaching the upper 70s by then.",
